From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] nvme: panic when a disk is marked dead

The link can come back while the disk doesn't. If the reset that follows
a resume fails, or the one after a timeout at runtime, nvme_reset_work()
marks the namespaces dead, with the same outcome as losing the link: ext4
is shut down on /data and /metadata, nothing in the kernel reboots the
system, and the controller refuses to suspend.

This machine has one NVMe device and everything runs from it, so panic
there. A failed reset, a reset that loses all I/O queues and the removal of
a controller that is already dead all go through
nvme_mark_namespaces_dead(). A namespace removed after a successful reset
doesn't, so that case isn't covered. The first probe doesn't either, so a
disk that never came up behaves as before, and neither does an orderly
removal.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [device-specific policy]
---
 drivers/nvme/host/core.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/nvme/host/core.c b/drivers/nvme/host/core.c
index a73cc6a43..993a705ea 100644
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/core.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/core.c
@@ -5298,6 +5298,12 @@ void nvme_mark_namespaces_dead(struct nvme_ctrl *ctrl)
 	struct nvme_ns *ns;
 	int srcu_idx;
 
+	/*
+	 * This is the only NVMe device in the machine and everything runs from
+	 * it. Nothing restarts the system once its disk is gone.
+	 */
+	panic("%s: disk lost\n", dev_name(ctrl->device));
+
 	srcu_idx = srcu_read_lock(&ctrl->srcu);
 	list_for_each_entry_srcu(ns, &ctrl->namespaces, list,
 				 srcu_read_lock_held(&ctrl->srcu))
